 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
--   **Mesh Dependency**: Alternating vertices have a different number of tetrahedra intersecting on them, resulting in a slightly different mesh density depending on the position relative to the grid origin [S1, S2].
--   **Artifacts**: The choice of tetrahedron orientation may produce artificial "bumps" in the isosurface due to interpolation along the face diagonals [S1, S2].
--   **Near Points Inside Surface**: The technique does not address the case where two near points 'inside' a surface are a combined distance < 1 from the surface; they should generate two points on the edge instead of 1. This limitation is related to Manifold Dual Contouring [S1, S2].
